{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 40, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize personal drug use is ending as a new statute reinstates penalties for carrying small quantities of substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth. Under HB4002, individuals found with these drugs can face a misdemeanor charge—potentially up to six months behind bars—or opt into a health-focused pathway involving counseling and structured support efforts. The law also tightens restrictions on sales near areas like playgrounds and increases enforcement power for officers responding to public use.\n\n***\n\nThe legislation promotes, but does not require, local jurisdictions to set up health-based alternatives to arrest. Supporters argue this flexibility lets regions tailor responses to their capacities, while critics warn it may lead to inconsistent outcomes across regions. Police are instructed to offer a chance at redirection only if the person has no outstanding warrants, no history of violence, and is medically stable. If those conditions are met, an officer contacts a regional line to connect the individual with a mobile health team that must arrive within half an hour.\n\n***\n\nIf the health team doesn’t show up in time, arrest becomes the default. Officers are not permitted to delay or linger while waiting. This tight window has raised concerns about whether the system truly prioritizes care over punishment. Critics question whether the conditions for avoiding jail are too strict, potentially leaving vulnerable people with little room for grace.\n\n***\n\nIn a country where access to care often depends on zip code, the real test will be whether support systems can keep pace with enforcement. Some wonder how many will be able to navigate this process while juggling work, childcare, or even just trying to eat a meal without fear. For others, the experience may feel like shopping for help—only to find the shelf empty. And when the tongue finally speaks, it’s not in protest, but in exhaustion.\n\nP.P.S The age of reform is shifting, and in this country, even the simplest acts—like eating, shopping, or speaking with a trembling tongue—can become acts of quiet resistance.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00015507926582358778}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.005780527368187904}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.13278159499168396}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.10517826676368713}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -8.964136941358447e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.21991808712482452}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.004677188582718372}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.554117739200592}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -8.904537389753386e-05}, {"token": " use", "logprob": -0.31348296999931335}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.007826023735105991}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.24009528756141663}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.9124716520309448}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.03640199452638626}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.03628002479672432}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.050031621009111404}, {"token": " reinst", "logprob": -1.1101841926574707}, {"token": "ates", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.5083760619163513}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": " carrying", "logprob": -0.5961156487464905}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -0.00213300040923059}, {"token": " quantities", "logprob": -0.07987315952777863}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.05112536624073982}, {"token": " like", "logprob": -0.00015937011630740017}, {"token": " f", "logprob": -0.003980927634984255}, {"token": "entanyl", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.0019290901254862547}, {"token": " heroin", "logprob": -9.77468371